  "sn22.55:1.3": "I follow the interpretation established by Bhikkhu Bodhi, for which see his note 75 on this discourse in <em>Connected Discourses</em>. When using first person pronouns, the formula epitomizes the annihilationist view: “I might not be” (<a href='https://suttacentral.net/sn22.81/en/sujato#11.9'>SN 22.81:11.9</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/sn24.4/en/sujato#1.3'>SN 24.4:1.3</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/sn22.153/en/sujato#1.3'>SN 22.153:1.3</a>, <a href='https://suttacentral.net/an10.29/en/sujato#19.1'>AN 10.29:19.1</a>). The Buddha reformed it with the impersonal third person pronoun: “It might not be”. ",
